    "gatesRetired": {
      "2026-08-16": {
        "scripts/qa/test-legacy-coordination-decommission.sh": "Retired, not repealed. Six assertions, four of which re-ran another CI job: four `cargo test` filters the `test` job already runs, `coverage-governance.sh --fixture-test` which has its own job, and `test-coordination-strangler.sh` in full, which is its own job. The governance job paid 186s per push for the repeat — its second most expensive step against a 1444s total. The fifth assertion, the frozen inventory expectation, is coordination-governance.rb's subject and it runs as its own gate. The sixth was the only one nothing else made — that the scheduler's item_executor and loop_engine hold no capture/JSONPath extraction path — and it moved verbatim into test-coordination-strangler.sh, whose subject it is.",
        "scripts/qa/test-pipeline-variable-retirement.sh": "Retired. 529 lines and an isolated daemon per push, re-proving a migration FR-156 completed once; measured at 2s in the cost ledger, so the saving is a human's attention rather than CI time. Its bundle stays: fixtures/manifests/bundles/fr156-pipeline-variable-parity.yaml is still asserted to be rejected by its own declared diagnostics, by core's every_tracked_bundle_is_accepted_or_declared, which derives its corpus from `git ls-files` and enumerates nothing."
      },
      "$rule": "DD-172 §3 states the retirement rule and leaves N unset, waiting for the freshness ledger to accumulate enough history to choose it from evidence. That wait cannot end: gate_runlog.sh writes `entry[\"lastRun\"] = {...}`, a single object overwritten on every run, so the ledger holds one record per gate and never N. These two are retired on the other evidence DD-172 names — the subject, not the catch rate. A gate whose work another job already does, and a gate whose migration completed, are both answerable today."
    }
